Page
    Role: Content
    Purpose: Research concept for using STF as a structural intermediate representation for executable agent logic.

    Section
        Role: PageHeading
        Text
            Content: SIR (Semantic Intermediate Representation)
        Text
            Content: A Structural Model for Agent-Native Executable Logic
        Text
            Content: Version 0.2.0-draft (conceptual)

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Abstract and motivation.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Abstract
        Text
            Content: This document explores the use of STF as a general-purpose intermediate representation (IR) for executable logic, designed specifically for intelligent agents and compilers rather than for human readability.
        Text
            Content: The language problem — traditional programming languages were designed for humans. They carry syntactic sugar, context-dependent grammars, and formatting conventions. When LLMs generate code, they waste tokens predicting syntax rather than semantics, and they are prone to syntax hallucinations. Furthermore, combining modules from different languages (e.g., Python and C) is complex due to mismatched intermediate representations and foreign function interfaces.
        Text
            Content: Proposed direction — use STF as a direct Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) representation. Agents write structural nodes directly, making syntax errors structurally impossible. SIR allows modules to exist at different levels of abstraction within the same semantic graph, bridging the gap between high-level logic and low-level execution.
        Text
            Content: This document does not define a production-ready compiler, instruction set architecture, runtime memory manager, or type system. It is a research starting point intended to invite independent implementations and alternative models.
        Text
            Content: Status — conceptual draft. The language is descriptive, not normative.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Core design thesis and principles.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Design Thesis
        Text
            Content: Executable agent logic should be represented as a structural semantic tree rather than a flat text string parsed by context-dependent grammars.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Core Principles
        Text
            Content: Principle 1 — Code is Data. SIR modules are valid STF documents. They can be read, written, and reasoned about by agents as semantic trees, not as raw text strings.
        Text
            Content: Principle 2 — Explicit Abstraction. SIR modules may be written at different levels of abstraction (High, Mid, Low). The level is declared explicitly in the structure, allowing the compiler to understand the context without guessing.
        Text
            Content: Principle 3 — Unified Memory Space. Executable code (SIR) and persistent state (STF-Memory) share the same structural graph and addressing scheme. The boundary between application data and executable logic becomes structurally transparent.
        Text
            Content: Principle 4 — Trust-Aware Execution. SIR reuses the STF Trust Model. Code execution authority is bounded by Origin. Untrusted code (from agents) is structurally forbidden from executing side-effects.
        Text
            Content: Principle 5 — Structural Determinism. Control flow and concurrency are represented as explicit structural branches, avoiding hidden state mutations or ambiguous jumps.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Conceptual model of execution and memory.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Conceptual Model
        Text
            Content: SIR models executable logic as a semantic tree whose nodes represent functions, instructions, variables, and control flow branches.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: State Isolation (Transient vs Persistent)
        Text
            Content: SIR explicitly separates execution state from persistent memory. Transient Memory Nodes are local variables and intermediate calculations that live only within a function's execution frame and are destroyed upon Return. Persistent Memory Nodes (STF-Memory) are long-lived graph nodes. A Tree-Walk runtime MUST isolate Transient scopes to prevent the pollution of the persistent knowledge graph with temporary data.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Multi-Level Unification
        Text
            Content: Traditional compilers separate High-Level IR (HIR), Mid-Level IR (MIR), and Low-Level IR (LIR). SIR proposes that these levels can coexist within the same STF representation. An agent may write a high-level functional call, which invokes a low-level memory manipulation instruction.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Structural ABI Contracts
        Text
            Content: When a High-Level node invokes a Low-Level instruction, the compiler requires an explicit boundary contract. SIR defines Structural ABI nodes where high-level data structures are unpacked into primitive representations (e.g., raw memory offsets or register mappings) via explicit mapping. Without this contract, cross-level lowering to targets like WebAssembly is structurally unresolvable and ambiguous.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Execution Phases
        Text
            Content: A SIR program is intended to be executed in a dual-phase model. Phase 1 is Tree-Walk Interpretation, where the runtime directly interprets the STF AST for rapid agent prototyping. Phase 2 is AOT Compilation, where the structural tree is lowered to a binary format for production performance.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Conceptual operations.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Conceptual Operations
        Text
            Content: The following operations are proposed as conceptual primitives. They are not part of the STF grammar and are not normative.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Call
        Text
            Content: Invokes a function node, passing parameters and receiving a result. Calls may cross abstraction levels seamlessly, provided Structural ABI contracts are satisfied.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Branch
        Text
            Content: Evaluates a condition and structurally executes one of two (or more) child blocks. Prevents arbitrary jumps.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: AsyncGroup
        Text
            Content: Declares that a set of instructions have no data dependencies and may be executed concurrently. Synchronization is explicit.

        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Result Return
        Text
            Content: Functions return a monadic Result node rather than throwing exceptions. Callers inspect the structural State (Ok or Error) to handle failure, preventing hidden stack unwinding.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Explicit trade-offs and limitations.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Trade-offs and Limitations
        Text
            Content: The Syntax vs Verbosity Trade-off — representing an AST in textual STF format requires explicit indentation and metadata nodes. This inevitably increases token usage compared to flat, syntactically dense languages like Python. This cost is accepted intentionally, trading token efficiency for structural determinism, zero syntax hallucinations, and machine-verifiable semantics.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Relationship to the STF Ecosystem.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Ecosystem Closure
        Text
            Content: SIR is an application vocabulary of the STF ecosystem. STF provides the syntax. SIL provides the human interface. STF-Memory provides the persistent state. SIR provides the executable logic. Together, they form a machine-readable environment for autonomous agents where data, memory, interface, and code share a single structural language.
        Text
            Content: The vocabulary set is deliberately open: further application vocabularies on the same core (e.g., configuration languages, frontend/interface-generation languages) are expected and desirable. A new domain joins by defining its vocabulary over the STF core — not by extending the core grammar.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: How this concept relates to the normative specification.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Status Relative to the Specification
        Text
            Content: This concept preceded and motivated the normative specification of SIR. Where this document and the specification differ, the specification governs; this document remains the design rationale and research agenda.
        Text
            Content: Several proposals of this concept are now normative: structural Branch/Loop control flow, the monadic Result model, dual-phase execution (tree-walk interpretation and AOT lowering), explicit abstraction levels with a declared ladder (High / Mid / Low), a two-domain value model separating portable boxed values from raw linear-memory quantities, structural ABI contracts at level boundaries (held as a reserved contract node until its concrete structure is standardized), and native module composition via includes.
        Text
            Content: Remaining research territory: structural concurrency (AsyncGroup is held reserved and inert until its semantics are designed) and dynamic generation of ABI contracts across levels. Self-hosting — open question Q4 — has moved from research to engineering: a SIR-to-binary compiler written in SIR exists and is being driven to a bootstrap fixpoint as its acceptance milestone.

    Section
        Role: Content
        Purpose: Open research questions.
        Heading
            Type: Heading
            Content: Open Research Questions
        Text
            Content: Q1 — can LLMs effectively generate pure AST structures (SIR) with lower hallucination rates compared to text-based code generation, despite the higher token overhead?
        Text
            Content: Q2 — what is the minimal viable instruction set (Op vocabulary) that covers general computation while remaining semantic for agents?
        Text
            Content: Q3 — how does structural concurrency (AsyncGroup) compare to traditional thread/async models in terms of compiler lowering complexity?
        Text
            Content: Q4 — can a SIR compiler be fully self-hosted (written in SIR) using only the STF grammar?
        Text
            Content: Q5 — what is the optimal strategy for generating Structural ABI Contracts dynamically when agent code crosses abstraction levels?